/-!
# Finite vector data for modular faithfulness

The displayed rows form a finite invariant-set candidate for the two literal
reduced Eisenstein generators. A base-four digit `a + 2b` denotes `a + bω` in
GF(4). The bit mask is only a membership sieve: the proof checks every indexed
point against it and uses it to exclude images of three distinguished vectors.
No completeness, orbit cardinality, or group recognition is assumed.

For each of e₂, e₄, e₅, three translates by the invariant row space distinguish
that vector among all indexed points. Together with those three rows, these
vectors give an invertible frame. `Certificates` checks the numerical identities;
`ModularFaithfulness` gives the linear argument and the actual kernel bound.

Numerical source: John Bray's ATLAS representation (row convention),
https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/lin/L34/mag/6L34G1-Ar6aB0.M .
`Source/generate.py` reproduces the candidate using only modular arithmetic.
All assertions about the generated data are proved by Lean, independently of
any integral orbit or sign-kernel result.
-/

